{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed2094082456/10/codegen_prompts/5.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2094082456/10`\n- 题号：`5`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed2094082456/10/5.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"5\">\n- `description`：识别网格中由不同颜色构成的\"路径段\"和\"门\"像素，路径段通过颜色匹配的门相连，最终将整条路径拉直为一条水平线并按通过顺序排列各段颜色。\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为黑色。\n  - 网格中有若干水平或垂直的单像素宽彩色线段（路径段），每段是单一颜色，各段颜色不同。\n  - 每个路径段的一端或两端紧邻一个\"门\"像素（与路径段正交方向相邻，4连通）。门像素的颜色不是该路径段的颜色，而是另一条路径段的颜色，表示两条路径段通过这个门相连。\n  - 整个网格中，所有路径段通过门像素形成一条不分叉的链：起始段只有一端有门，末尾段也只有一端有门，中间段两端各有一个门。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 找到起始路径段（只有一端连有门像素的段）。\n  - 从起始段出发，通过门像素的颜色找到下一段路径段，依此类推，遍历整条链。\n  - 输出为一行像素：按遍历顺序，每条路径段贡献其长度个像素（颜色为该段颜色），各段之间用一个灰色像素分隔。\n- `Key insight`：门像素的颜色是指向下一段的\"指针\"，解题者必须先理解门像素的链接语义（颜色匹配目标段），再沿链遍历，最后将空间中弯折分散的路径线性化。关键在于门的颜色编码的是拓扑连接关系而非空间位置。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：train/458e3a53, train/17829a00, train/2601afb7, train/57edb29d, eval/62593bfd, train/9f669b64, train/a1aa0c1e, eval/9385bd28\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"458e3a53\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3866\nmechanism=0.4762\nlexical=0.0142\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:axis, object:line_segment, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:crop_resize, operation:sort_order, output:new_canvas, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/458e3a53.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/458e3a53.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtract the compact table of solid cells hidden inside a separator lattice.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has full-width and full-height bars of one separator color, dividing it into rectangular cells.\n- Most cells contain a repeating multicolor pattern, while a contiguous rectangle of cells is solid monochrome.\n\nRule\n- Split the input into lattice cells using the full separator rows and columns.\n- Keep the cells whose full interior is one color, crop to their occupied rectangle, and output those colors.\n\nKey insight\n- The periodic pattern is noise; only completely solid lattice cells encode the reduced output grid.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"solid monochrome cells inside a full-row/full-column separator lattice over a periodic pattern\",\n  \"dst\": \"compact rectangle of solid-cell colors, preserving lattice row and column order\",\n  \"transform\": \"split at separator bars; identify cells whose interior is a single color; crop to the occupied solid-cell rectangle and replace each cell by its color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"height and width equal to the occupied solid-cell rows and columns\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"17829a00\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3454\nmechanism=0.4283\nlexical=0.0136\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:line_segment, object:path, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:adjacent, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/17829a00.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/17829a00.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove each colored object vertically until it touches the matching top or bottom border bar.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The top row is a full-width bar in one foreground color and the bottom row is a full-width bar in another.\n- All other foreground pixels are 8-connected monochrome objects whose color matches one of those bars.\n\nRule:\n- Top-bar-colored objects move upward so their bbox top is row 1.\n- Bottom-bar-colored objects move downward so their bbox bottom is the row above the bottom bar.\n- A downward-moving one-column segment whose landing overlaps its source becomes the continuous vertical segment swept between them.\n\nKey insight:\nThe horizontal bars are destination anchors: color determines which border an object slides toward, while columns and object shape are otherwise preserved.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"non-border 8-connected foreground objects matching a full-width border bar color\",\n  \"path\": \"vertical path toward the same-color top or bottom border bar\",\n  \"dst\": \"touch the same-color border bar\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"translate each object to the adjacent row of that bar; a downward one-column segment whose landing overlaps its source fills the continuous swept segment\",\n  \"transform\": \"redraw the border bars and moved objects on the bg\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2601afb7\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3360\nmechanism=0.3721\nlexical=0.0148\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:line_segment, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/2601afb7.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/2601afb7.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRotate each vertical bar's color one slot right and its height one slot left.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background surrounds several monochrome one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Every bar is bottom-aligned and occupies a distinct column.\n- The ordered bar slots are read from left to right.\n\nRule\n- Keep the grid shape and the bar columns unchanged.\n- At slot i, use the color from the previous slot cyclically.\n- At slot i, use the height from the next slot cyclically.\n\nKey insight\n- Color and height are independent cyclic attributes of the ordered bar slots:\n  colors rotate right while heights rotate left.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"bottom-aligned one-cell-wide vertical bars ordered left-to-right\",\n  \"transform\": \"redraw each bar in the same column with the previous bar's color cyclically and the next bar's height cyclically\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"57edb29d\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3215\nmechanism=0.3511\nlexical=0.0137\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, code:output_resize, object:axis, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, operation:shape_match, operation:sort_order, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/57edb29d.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/57edb29d.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtract the single plain rectangle and redraw it with the aligned rectangles' border-relative marker pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- The boundary color is the background/separator.\n- Foreground objects are dense rectangular regions; exactly one rectangle has only its base color.\n- Other rectangles contain their base color plus marker pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop the only plain rectangle; this crop is the output canvas.\n- Use marked rectangles whose row span overlaps the plain rectangle's row span.\n- Transfer their marker pixels by preserving each marker run's distance from the nearest rectangle edges.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marked rectangles are examples of an edge-relative pattern, so the missing plain rectangle receives the same pattern resized to its own dimensions.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"edge-relative marker run s in a marked rectangle aligned with the plain rectangle row band\",\n  \"dst\": \"run d in the plain rectangle crop\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.nearest_edge_offsets == d.nearest_edge_offsets\",\n  \"transform\": \"paint each matched d with the shared marker color; keep base rectangle fill elsewhere\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same shape as the plain rectangle\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3303\nmechanism=0.3346\nlexical=0.0137\ncode_feature=0.8333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:shape_match, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/62593bfd.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/62593bfd.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"all connected components (objects) on the background\",\n  \"dst\": \"translate each object d vertically to an edge (x preserved)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"group objects by their pixel-count (area). If an area value occurs >=2 times, those objects go to the TOP edge; if the area is unique, that object goes to the BOTTOM edge\",\n  \"transform\": \"duplicated-size -> top; singleton-size -> bottom\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f669b64\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3162\nmechanism=0.3435\nlexical=0.0133\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:axis, object:line_segment, object:path, object:sprite, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9f669b64.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/9f669b64.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nPush the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one\n  horizontal or vertical axis.\n- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.\n- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular\n  to the movement axis; this is the target to split.\n\nRule\n- Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is\n  flush with the grid edge in that direction.\n- Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.\n- Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and\n  shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.\n- Keep the other end object unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels\n  away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
